\title{Combinatorics of the Universal Library:\\
Counting, Diagonalization, and de Bruijn Catalogs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Borges' \emph{Library of Babel} is the set of all strings of a fixed length over a
finite alphabet---a finite but astronomically large universal information space. We
give a rigorous combinatorial treatment of three questions about such spaces:
(i) \emph{how rare is a given pattern?}, (ii) \emph{can the space catalog itself?},
and (iii) \emph{can a complete, addressable catalog be constructed efficiently?} For
the first, we prove an exact fixed-position occurrence count: the number of volumes
agreeing with a prescribed pattern on $m$ positions is exactly $b^{L-m}$, giving a
per-position match probability of $b^{-m}$ with \emph{no} length prefactor; the
spurious linear factor in the folklore estimate is identified as the union-bound
window count $L-m+1$ of the distinct ``occurs-anywhere'' problem, corrected by the
string's autocorrelation. For the second, a pigeonhole/diagonal argument shows no
single volume can index all $b^L$ volumes, while a distributed catalog over $N$
volumes can do so \emph{iff} $N \ge b^L/L$ (reference-count model), a threshold that
climbs to $N \gtrsim b^L$ under faithful bit encoding. For the third, we cast catalog
construction as the de Bruijn window bijection: a sequence whose order-$n$ windows
enumerate all $b^n$ words must have length exactly $b^n$, and such sequences exist and
are constructible in time linear in their length via an Eulerian circuit in the de
Bruijn graph. We illustrate with the Borges parameters $(b,L)=(25,1{,}312{,}000)$ and a
mini-Library $(b,n)=(4,16)$. All central claims correspond to formally verified
statements: \texttt{card\_agree\_on}, \texttt{prob\_match},
\texttt{distributed\_catalog\_iff}, \texttt{IsDeBruijn}, \texttt{window}, and
\texttt{isDeBruijn\_length}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

In \emph{The Library of Babel} (1941), Jorge Luis Borges describes a universe
consisting of all books of a fixed format: 410 pages, 40 lines per page, 80
characters per line, over an alphabet of 25 orthographic symbols. Every such book
exists exactly once. The Library is therefore the set of all strings of length
$L = 1{,}312{,}000$ over an alphabet of size $b = 25$, of cardinality
$b^L = 25^{1{,}312{,}000}$.

The object Borges defined is a clean mathematical one: a \emph{universal information
space}, the free monoid restricted to a fixed length. Such spaces recur throughout
mathematics and computer science---all files of a given size, all genomes of a given
length, all keys of a given width, all fixed-architecture weight vectors. Three
questions are fundamental:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Occurrence/rarity.} How many volumes contain a given target, and with
        what probability?
  \item \textbf{Self-cataloging (diagonalization).} Can a volume---or a bounded family
        of volumes---encode the address of every volume, including itself?
  \item \textbf{Constructive cataloging.} Can one efficiently build a single object
        listing every possible short passage exactly once, with computable addresses?
\end{enumerate}
We answer all three exactly, correcting in particular the belief that pattern-match
probability carries a linear length factor.

\subsection{Notation}
Fix $b \ge 1$ and $L \ge 0$. The \emph{Library} is
\[
  \Lib(b,L) \;=\; \{0,1,\dots,b-1\}^{L} \;\cong\; (\Fin\,b)^{\Fin\,L},
\]
with $|\Lib(b,L)| = b^L$. A \emph{volume} is $v \in \Lib(b,L)$. A \emph{pattern on}
$S \subseteq \{0,\dots,L-1\}$ is $p : S \to \{0,\dots,b-1\}$; a \emph{target word} of
length $m$ is $T \in \{0,\dots,b-1\}^m$.

\section{The counting law}

\subsection{Exact agreement count}

\begin{definition}[agreement set]
For $S \subseteq \{0,\dots,L-1\}$ and $p : S \to \{0,\dots,b-1\}$,
\[
  \Agree(p) \;=\; \{\, v \in \Lib(b,L) : v(i) = p(i)\ \text{for all } i \in S \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{card\_agree\_on}]\label{thm:agree}
Let $|S| = m$. Then $|\Agree(p)| = b^{\,L-m}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A volume in $\Agree(p)$ is determined by its values on the $L-m$ positions outside
$S$, those on $S$ being forced to equal $p$. The restriction map
$v \mapsto v|_{\{0,\dots,L-1\}\setminus S}$ is a bijection from $\Agree(p)$ onto
$\{0,\dots,b-1\}^{L-m}$: injective because $v$ is determined on $S$ by $p$ and on the
complement by its restriction; surjective because any choice on the complement extends
uniquely. Hence $|\Agree(p)| = b^{L-m}$.
\end{proof}

Taking $S = \{j,\dots,j+m-1\}$ and $p$ the symbols of a target $T$ counts volumes
carrying $T$ at fixed position $j$: $b^{L-m}$, whenever $j+m \le L$.

\subsection{Fixed-position match probability}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{prob\_match}]\label{thm:prob}
Under the uniform distribution on $\Lib(b,L)$, for any target $T$ of length $m \le L$
and any fixed admissible position $j$,
\[
  \Pr[\, v \text{ matches } T \text{ at } j \,] \;=\; \frac{b^{\,L-m}}{b^{L}} \;=\; b^{-m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide the agreement count $b^{L-m}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:agree}) by the total $b^L$.
Equivalently, the $m$ window positions are independent uniform symbols, each matching
with probability $1/b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[no length prefactor]
The folklore estimate ``$\Pr \approx |T|\cdot b^{-m}$'' is false as a per-position
statement: Theorem~\ref{thm:prob} gives the pure exponential $b^{-m}$. The linear
factor belongs to a different event.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The ``occurs anywhere'' correction}

Let $\Occ(T) = \{ v : T \text{ occurs as a contiguous substring of } v \}$, with
$W = L-m+1$ candidate windows.

\begin{proposition}[windowed bounds]\label{prop:window}
For $m \le L$,
\[
  (L-m+1)\,b^{\,L-m} - \binom{L-m+1}{2}\, b^{\,L-2m+c(T)}
  \;\le\; |\Occ(T)| \;\le\; (L-m+1)\,b^{\,L-m},
\]
where the second-order term is governed by the autocorrelation $c(T)$ of $T$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The upper bound is a union bound: $\Occ(T)=\bigcup_j A_j$, $A_j=$ ``$T$ at $j$'',
$|A_j| = b^{L-m}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:agree}, over $L-m+1$ windows. The first
inclusion--exclusion correction subtracts pairwise intersections $|A_j \cap A_{j'}|$;
for overlapping windows the joint constraint is encoded by the Guibas--Odlyzko
correlation polynomial of $T$. Non-self-overlapping targets have trivial correlation
and tightest bound. Dividing by $b^L$ gives $\Pr[\Occ(T)] \le (L-m+1)b^{-m}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Scale of the Borges Library}
With $b=25$, $L=1{,}312{,}000$: $|\Lib| = 25^{1{,}312{,}000}$ has
$\lfloor L\log_{10}b\rfloor+1 = 1{,}834{,}098$ decimal digits; a fixed 100-symbol
passage occurs at a fixed position in a fraction $25^{-100}\approx 10^{-140}$ of
volumes; one volume holds $L\log_2 b \approx 6.09\times10^{6}$ bits.

\section{Diagonalization: can the Library catalog itself?}

A \emph{catalog} provides, for each of the $b^L$ volumes, a decodable reference. Model
a catalog as a family of volumes, each supplying its $L$ positions as reference slots.

\subsection{Single-volume impossibility}

\begin{theorem}[no total book]\label{thm:single}
If $b^L > L$, there is no injection $\Lib(b,L)\hookrightarrow\{0,\dots,L-1\}$; no single
volume can store a distinct reference for every volume.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A single volume has $L$ positions, hence at most $L$ distinct one-symbol references.
An injection from a set of size $b^L$ into one of size $L$ requires $b^L \le L$
(pigeonhole). For Borges-scale parameters $b^L \gg L$, contradiction.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Distributed catalogs: an exact threshold}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{distributed\_catalog\_iff}]\label{thm:dist}
A distributed catalog of $N$ volumes can assign every volume a distinct reference slot
iff
\[
  N \cdot L \;\ge\; b^{L}, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad N \;\ge\; \frac{b^{L}}{L}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) If $NL \ge b^L$, the $b^L$ volumes inject into the $NL$ slots via
Euclidean division of the volume index into (volume, position); injectivity is
uniqueness of division with remainder. ($\Rightarrow$) If $NL < b^L$, pigeonhole
forbids the injection, so some volume is unlisted. The threshold
$N \ge \lceil b^L/L\rceil$ is exact.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[catalog $\approx$ Library]
The minimal distributed catalog has $\lceil b^L/L\rceil$ volumes; for
$(b,L)=(25,1312000)$ this has about $1{,}834{,}092$ decimal digits---six shy of the
Library. The map is nearly the size of the territory.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Bit-faithful encoding}

\begin{conjecture}[entropy lower bound]\label{conj:entropy}
Under bit-faithful encoding, each reference costs $\lceil L\log_2 b\rceil$ bits, so a
distributed catalog requires $\ge b^L\lceil L\log_2 b\rceil$ bits, i.e.\ $N \gtrsim b^L$
volumes---a factor $L\log_2 b$ above Theorem~\ref{thm:dist}.
\end{conjecture}

This is a Kraft-inequality statement: $b^L$ distinct prefix-free binary codewords force
average length $\ge \log_2 b^L$. The combinatorial cost is $b^L/L$; the informational
cost is $b^L$.

\section{Constructive catalogs via de Bruijn sequences}

\subsection{The window map}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{window}]
For a cyclic $s : \Fin\,N \to \{0,\dots,b-1\}$, the order-$n$ window map is
\[
  \window_s : \Fin\,N \to \{0,\dots,b-1\}^{n}, \qquad
  \window_s(i) = \big(s(i),s(i+1),\dots,s(i+n-1)\big)\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{IsDeBruijn}]
$s$ is a de Bruijn sequence of order $n$ over $b$ symbols, $\IsDeBruijn(b,n,s)$, iff
$\window_s$ is a bijection onto $\{0,\dots,b-1\}^{n}$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Forced length}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{isDeBruijn\_length}]\label{thm:length}
If $\IsDeBruijn(b,n,s)$ for $s : \Fin\,N \to \{0,\dots,b-1\}$, then $N = b^{n}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A bijection equates domain and codomain cardinalities: $N = |\Fin\,N| =
|\{0,\dots,b-1\}^n| = b^n$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Existence and construction}

\begin{theorem}[existence]\label{thm:exist}
For all $b,n \ge 1$, a de Bruijn sequence of order $n$ over $b$ symbols exists and is
constructible in time $O(b^n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Form the de Bruijn graph $G_{b,n}$: vertices are the $b^{n-1}$ words of length $n-1$;
for each length-$n$ word $w_1\cdots w_n$ an edge $w_1\cdots w_{n-1}\to w_2\cdots w_n$
labeled $w$. Every vertex has in-/out-degree $b$, so $G_{b,n}$ is balanced and strongly
connected; by Euler's theorem it has an Eulerian circuit traversing each edge once. The
edge-label sequence is a cyclic string of length $b^n$ whose order-$n$ windows are the
$b^n$ edges, each once---hence de Bruijn. Hierholzer's algorithm finds the circuit in
$O(b^n)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The mini-Library $B(4,16)$}
For $b=4$, $n=16$ the construction yields a cyclic sequence of length
$4^{16} = 2^{32} = 4{,}294{,}967{,}296$ in which each 16-symbol block appears once, at
address $\window^{-1}$.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Occurrence count} (\texttt{card\_agree\_on}): return $b^{L-m}$ and
        $b^{-m}$; $O(1)$ big-integer arithmetic.
  \item \textbf{Catalog threshold} (\texttt{distributed\_catalog\_iff}): return
        $\lceil b^L/L\rceil$ and bit-faithful $\sim b^L$; $O(1)$ big-integer ops.
  \item \textbf{de Bruijn construction}: Hierholzer on $G_{b,n}$; emit length-$b^n$
        sequence; verify $\window$ is a bijection. Time/space $O(b^n)$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications and discussion}

Theorem~\ref{thm:prob} quantifies why brute-force discovery in universal spaces is
infeasible. Theorem~\ref{thm:single} is a combinatorial sibling of Cantor/Gödel
diagonalization and compression lower bounds. de Bruijn sequences
(Theorem~\ref{thm:exist}) power positional encoders, genome assembly, and pseudorandom
constructions. A unifying theme is \emph{map versus territory}: the per-symbol cost of
pinning content is fixed; the self-catalog is nearly the whole Library; the optimal
$n$-gram catalog is exactly $b^n$ long.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{description}
  \item[C1.] Sharpen Theorem~\ref{thm:prob} to the two-sided
    Proposition~\ref{prop:window} via the Guibas--Odlyzko correlation polynomial.
  \item[C2.] Promote Theorem~\ref{thm:dist} to the bit-faithful Kraft bound
    (Conjecture~\ref{conj:entropy}), raising the threshold from $b^L/L$ to $\sim b^L$.
  \item[C3.] Formalize Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} as Eulerian-circuit existence in
    $G_{b,n}$, realizing $B(4,16)$, complementing \texttt{isDeBruijn\_length}.
  \item[C4.] Study the probability that a random Borges volume encodes a valid proof of
    a target theorem, conjecturing a sharp threshold in proof complexity.
\end{description}

